---
title: LDL Specification
subtitle: Normative specification of the loclass Document Language
author: loclass project
version: 0.1.0
date: 2026-07-10
language: en
revision: 1
---

chapter
  LDL Specification

LDL stands for loclass Document Language.

LDL is a semantic markup language for structured documents. It describes the
logical meaning and organization of document content independently of a
concrete output format.

A renderer translates an LDL document into an output format such as PDF, HTML,
DOCX or ODT.

This document is the normative specification of LDL.

section
  Status of This Specification

This file is the single normative source for the LDL language.

Other documents, including README files, user documentation, generated
Markdown files and implementation notes, are informative only.

If another document contradicts this specification, this specification takes
precedence.

The parser, renderer, tests and user documentation MUST conform to this
specification.

section
  Normative Language

The key words MUST, MUST NOT, REQUIRED, SHALL, SHALL NOT, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT,
RECOMMENDED, MAY and OPTIONAL describe normative requirements.

MUST and MUST NOT define mandatory language rules.

SHOULD and SHOULD NOT define recommended behavior. An implementation may
deviate only when there is a justified reason.

MAY and OPTIONAL define permitted but non-mandatory behavior.

section
  Design Principles

LDL describes meaning rather than appearance.

LDL documents SHOULD remain readable as plain text.

Document content and document presentation MUST remain separate.

LDL MUST remain independent of a specific renderer or output format.

Language constructs SHOULD be explicit, predictable and easy to parse.

An LDL document has exactly one manifest.

Included LDL files contain document content only and MUST NOT contain a
manifest.

section
  Document Model

An LDL document consists of:

list
  params
    type: ordered

  body
    ---
    exactly one root document
    exactly one manifest
    zero or more document elements
    zero or more included content files
    ---

The root document is the file from which processing starts.

The root document owns the manifest.

Included files contribute document elements to the root document. They do not
form independent LDL documents.

The logical result of resolving all input directives is one document with one
manifest and one ordered sequence of document elements.

chapter
  Manifest

section
  Purpose

The manifest contains metadata and document-level configuration.

The manifest belongs to the complete LDL document, not to individual sections
or included files.

An LDL document MUST contain exactly one manifest.

section
  Position

The manifest MUST appear at the beginning of the root document.

The opening delimiter MUST be the first non-empty line of the root document.

No document content or directive may appear before the manifest.

An included file MUST NOT contain a manifest.

section
  Delimiters

A manifest begins with a line containing exactly three hyphen characters.

A manifest ends with another line containing exactly three hyphen characters.

Leading or trailing whitespace around a delimiter SHOULD NOT be used.

code
  params
    caption: Minimal manifest
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    ---
    ---
    ---

The opening and closing delimiters are not part of the manifest data.

section
  Field Syntax

Each manifest field consists of a field name, a colon and a scalar value.

code
  params
    caption: Manifest field syntax
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    field-name: value
    ---

Field names are case-sensitive.

Whitespace before and after the field name is ignored.

Whitespace before and after the value is ignored.

The first colon separates the field name from the value.

Additional colons are part of the value.

code
  params
    caption: Value containing colons
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    title: Service status: production
    ---

A manifest line that does not contain a colon is invalid.

section
  Scalar Values

The initial LDL manifest supports scalar values only.

Nested objects, sequences and multiline values are not part of the initial
manifest syntax.

An empty value is syntactically valid and represents an unset value.

code
  params
    caption: Empty manifest value
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    subtitle:
    ---

An implementation MAY warn when a field has an empty value.

section
  Duplicate Fields

A manifest field MUST NOT occur more than once.

The following manifest is invalid:

code
  params
    caption: Invalid duplicate field
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    ---
    title: First title
    title: Second title
    ---
    ---

An implementation MUST report a duplicate manifest field as an error.

The later value MUST NOT silently replace the earlier value.

section
  Unknown Fields

Only fields defined by this specification are valid manifest fields.

An unknown manifest field is an error.

Implementations MUST NOT silently ignore unknown fields.

This rule prevents spelling errors from becoming unnoticed configuration
errors.

section
  Defined Fields

The initial LDL manifest defines the following fields:

table
  params
    label: tab:manifest-fields
    caption: Defined manifest fields

  head
    Field | Required | Meaning

  body
    title | no | Document title
    subtitle | no | Document subtitle
    author | no | Document author
    company | no | Authoring organization
    customer | no | Intended customer or recipient
    language | no | Document language
    theme | no | Requested presentation theme
    version | no | Document version
    revision | no | Document revision
    date | no | Document date

No individual manifest field is required in the initial LDL specification.

The manifest itself remains mandatory.

section
  Title

The title field contains the primary document title.

code
  params
    caption: Title field
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    title: LDL Specification
    ---

A renderer SHOULD use the title as the principal document heading or document
metadata title.

section
  Subtitle

The subtitle field contains an optional secondary document title.

code
  params
    caption: Subtitle field
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    subtitle: Normative language reference
    ---

section
  Author

The author field identifies the author or responsible editor of the document.

code
  params
    caption: Author field
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    author: Frank Sieger
    ---

section
  Company

The company field identifies the organization responsible for creating or
publishing the document.

code
  params
    caption: Company field
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    company: Example GmbH
    ---

section
  Customer

The customer field identifies the intended customer, recipient or commissioning
organization.

code
  params
    caption: Customer field
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    customer: Example Customer
    ---

section
  Language

The language field identifies the primary language of the document.

code
  params
    caption: Language field
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    language: de
    ---

The initial specification does not prescribe a complete language-tag validation
scheme.

Implementations SHOULD accept common language identifiers such as de, en or
de-DE.

section
  Theme

The theme field identifies the requested presentation theme.

code
  params
    caption: Theme field
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    theme: default
    ---

A theme controls presentation and MUST NOT change the semantic meaning of the
document.

The availability and implementation of themes are renderer-specific.

section
  Version

The version field identifies the document version.

code
  params
    caption: Version field
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    version: 0.1.0
    ---

The initial specification treats the value as text and does not require
semantic versioning.

section
  Revision

The revision field identifies a revision within a document version.

code
  params
    caption: Revision field
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    revision: 1
    ---

The initial specification treats the value as text.

section
  Date

The date field identifies the document date.

code
  params
    caption: Date field
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    date: 2026-07-10
    ---

Dates SHOULD use the ISO 8601 calendar date form YYYY-MM-DD.

The initial specification does not require implementations to reject other
textual date representations.

section
  Complete Example

code
  params
    caption: Complete initial manifest
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    ---
    title: LDL Specification
    subtitle: Normative language reference
    author: Frank Sieger
    company: loclass
    customer:
    language: en
    theme: default
    version: 0.1.0
    revision: 1
    date: 2026-07-10
    ---
    ---

section
  Output Targets

Output formats are identified by user-facing artifact names such as pdf, html,
docx and odt.

Renderer technologies such as LaTeX, Pandoc or Typst are implementation details
and MUST NOT be used as output-format names.

A future revision of this specification is expected to define a targets
manifest field.

The initial manifest syntax supports scalar values only. Therefore, the targets
field is not yet part of the language.

Implementations MUST NOT introduce a temporary singular target field as a
substitute.

chapter
  Diagnostics

section
  General Rule

Diagnostics are implementation output and are not part of LDL document
content.

An implementation MUST report invalid LDL input clearly.

User-caused errors SHOULD be reported as structured LDL diagnostics rather than
raw implementation exceptions.

Internal implementation failures MAY produce a language-specific traceback for
debugging purposes.

section
  Diagnostic Format

A human-readable diagnostic SHOULD follow this structure:

code
  params
    caption: Structured LDL diagnostic
    language: text

  body
    ---
    [LDL:E001] document.ldl:1:1 Missing manifest.
    ---

A diagnostic SHOULD contain:

list
  params
    type: unordered

  body
    ---
    an LDL diagnostic prefix
    a stable diagnostic code
    the source file
    the source line
    the source column when available
    a concise explanation
    an optional corrective hint
    ---

Diagnostic output SHOULD remain suitable for filtering with standard
command-line tools.

section
  Manifest Diagnostics

The initial manifest diagnostics are:

table
  params
    label: tab:manifest-diagnostics
    caption: Manifest diagnostic codes

  head
    Code | Meaning

  body
    E001 | Missing manifest
    E002 | Unterminated manifest
    E003 | Invalid manifest line
    E004 | Duplicate manifest field
    E005 | Unknown manifest field
    E006 | Manifest in included file
    E007 | Manifest not at start of root document

Diagnostic codes are part of the implementation contract and SHOULD remain
stable once published.

chapter
  Document Structure

section
  Overview

An LDL document is an ordered sequence of document elements.

Document elements may be:

list
  params
    type: unordered

  body
    ---
    paragraphs
    headings
    input directives
    block directives
    ---

The manifest is document metadata and is not part of the document element sequence.

After resolving all input directives, the resulting document contains one manifest and one ordered sequence of document elements.

section
  Top-Level Elements

A top-level element begins at indentation level zero.

A non-empty line at indentation level zero begins a new document element.

Indented lines belong to the preceding directive when that directive permits nested content.

A parser MUST preserve the order of all document elements.

section
  Directive Names

A directive begins with its directive name on a line by itself.

Directive names are case-sensitive.

A directive name MUST appear at indentation level zero.

code
  params
    caption: Heading directive
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    section
      Document Structure
    ---

The content belonging to a directive is indented relative to the directive name.

section
  Directive Boundaries

A multiline directive ends before the next non-empty line at indentation level zero.

The next top-level line begins a new document element.

This rule applies regardless of whether the next element is another directive or a paragraph.

code
  params
    caption: Directive followed by a paragraph
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    list
      params
        type: unordered

      body
        ---
        first item
        second item
        ---

    This paragraph is not part of the list.
    ---

An implementation MUST NOT attach a following top-level paragraph to the preceding directive.

section
  Single-Value Directives

Some directives consist of a directive name followed by exactly one indented value line.

The initial single-value directives are:

list
  params
    type: unordered

  body
    ---
    chapter
    section
    subsection
    input
    ---

code
  params
    caption: Single-value directive
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    section
      Manifest
    ---

A single-value directive ends after its value line.

An empty value is invalid unless the directive specification explicitly allows it.

Additional indented lines are not part of the directive.

section
  Multiline Directives

A multiline directive may contain named sections such as params, head, body or file.

The sections supported by a directive are defined by that directive.

code
  params
    caption: Multiline directive
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    table
      params
        caption: Example table

      head
        Name | Value

      body
        Alpha | One
        Beta | Two
    ---

Section names are indented by two spaces relative to the directive name.

Content inside a section is indented by four spaces relative to the directive name unless the directive defines a different structure.

A directive MUST reject section names that are not defined for that directive.

section
  Paragraphs

Text that does not begin with a known directive name at indentation level zero is parsed as paragraph content.

A paragraph may contain one or more consecutive text lines.

code
  params
    caption: Paragraph
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    This is a paragraph.

    This is another paragraph.
    ---

A paragraph ends before the next known directive at indentation level zero.

Blank lines inside paragraph content are preserved according to the paragraph and raw-text rules defined later in this specification.

section
  Unknown Top-Level Names

A top-level line is interpreted as a directive only when its complete content matches a known directive name.

Other top-level text is paragraph content.

code
  params
    caption: Text resembling a directive
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    tables are useful in technical documentation.
    ---

The word tables does not match the directive name table and therefore remains paragraph content.

An unknown word at the beginning of a paragraph MUST NOT automatically produce an unknown-directive error.

section
  Indentation

The initial LDL specification uses spaces for indentation.

Tabs MUST NOT be used for structural indentation.

One indentation level consists of two space characters.

The standard indentation levels are:

table
  params
    label: tab:indentation-levels
    caption: Standard indentation levels

  head
    Level | Spaces | Meaning

  body
    0 | 0 | Top-level document element
    1 | 2 | Directive value or section name
    2 | 4 | Section content or parameter
    3 | 6 | Directive-specific nested content

Implementations MUST interpret structural indentation consistently.

An implementation SHOULD report inconsistent or unsupported indentation as an error rather than silently guessing the intended structure.

section
  Blank Lines

Blank lines may separate document elements.

Blank lines between top-level elements are not document elements themselves.

A parser MAY ignore leading and trailing blank lines around top-level elements.

Blank lines inside fenced bodies or raw content may be significant and MUST be handled according to the relevant directive specification.

section
  Element Order

Document elements remain in source order.

Input resolution replaces an input directive with the ordered elements of the included file.

The relative order of elements before and after an input directive MUST remain unchanged.

code
  params
    caption: Element order with input
    language: ldl

  body
    ---
    section
      Before

    input
      content/details.ldl

    section
      After
    ---

After input resolution, the elements from content/details.ldl appear between the sections Before and After.
